WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.400
This is what happened when the code was actually measured.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.800
A new crate whose only job is running the model and timing it.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.900
The first run said a smaller model was slower, and got everything wrong.

4
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:26.900
It was a faulty download. The same model, built properly, was perfect.

5
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.400
The output was the right shape either way. Shape is all the checking guarantees.

6
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.600
With a working copy, the small model is three times faster.

7
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:46.000
About thirty pieces of information per report instead of ten.

8
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.700
So the plan to use a small model for the easy work is worth keeping.

9
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:58.600
Then a third of answers stopped fitting the type they were meant to.

10
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:06.000
The number describing the limit was never passed on, so there was no limit.

11
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.100
Six failures in twenty became none. The bigger model had never tripped it.

12
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:18.100
The measuring tool had its own fault.

13
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:26.100
It counted three different problems as one, which would have blamed the wrong thing.

14
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.200
Separately: a job whose worker dies used to wait forever.

15
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:39.300
Nothing failed, so nothing was recorded. It now goes back in the queue.

16
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.400
Four faults, all found by running it rather than reading it.
